[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced jQuery Selectors: Attribute, Pseudo-Class, and Custom Selectors"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"jQuery provides powerful selectors that go beyond basic element selection. Advanced selectors allow you to target elements based on their attributes, pseudo-classes (states), or even custom criteria. This tutorial explores attribute selectors, pseudo-class selectors, and how to create custom selectors."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Attribute Selectors"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Attribute selectors target elements based on the presence or value of their attributes. They offer precise control over element selection."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[attribute]: Selects elements with the specified attribute (e.g., '[title]')."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[attribute=value]: Selects elements with the specified attribute and value (e.g., '[type="text"]')."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[attribute!=value]: Selects elements with the specified attribute but NOT the specified value (e.g., '[type!="password"]')."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[attribute^=value]: Selects elements with the specified attribute starting with the specified value (e.g., '[href^="https://"]')."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[attribute$=value]: Selects elements with the specified attribute ending with the specified value (e.g., '[src$=".jpg"]')."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[attribute*=value]: Selects elements with the specified attribute containing the specified value (e.g., '[title*="important"]')."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Example of attribute selectors
$('a[target="_blank"]').css('color', 'red'); // Selects all <a> tags with target="_blank" and changes their color to red.
$('input[type="text"]').val('Default Text'); // Selects all <input> tags with type="text" and sets their value to 'Default Text'.
$('img[alt]').attr('title', 'Image with alt text'); // Selects all <img> tags with an alt attribute and adds a title attribute."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Pseudo-Class Selectors"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pseudo-class selectors target elements based on their state or position within the DOM. They are denoted by a colon (:) followed by the pseudo-class name."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":":first-child: Selects the first child element of its parent."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":":last-child: Selects the last child element of its parent."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":":nth-child(n): Selects the nth child element of its parent (n is a number or keyword like 'even' or 'odd')."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":":hover: Selects elements that are being hovered over by the mouse."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":":focus: Selects elements that have focus (e.g., input fields)."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":":checked: Selects checked radio buttons or checkboxes."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"// Example of pseudo-class selectors
$('li:first-child').css('font-weight', 'bold'); // Selects the first <li> element in each <ul> or <ol> and makes it bold.
$('tr:even').css('background-color', '#f2f2f2'); // Selects even <tr> elements and sets their background color.
$('input[type="checkbox"]:checked').parent().css('border', '2px solid green'); // Selects checked checkboxes and adds a green border to their parent element."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Custom Selectors ($.expr[':'])"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"jQuery allows you to create your own custom selectors using $.expr[':']. This is useful when you need to select elements based on criteria not covered by the built-in selectors."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"// Example of creating a custom selector
$.expr[':'].containsCaseInsensitive = $.expr.createPseudo(function(arg) {
    return function(elem) {
        return $(elem).text().toUpperCase().indexOf(arg.toUpperCase()) >= 0;
    };
});

// Using the custom selector
$('div:containsCaseInsensitive("example")').css('background-color', 'yellow'); // Selects all <div> elements that contain the word "example" (case-insensitive) and sets their background color to yellow."},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we create a custom selector called ':containsCaseInsensitive' that selects elements whose text content contains a specific string, regardless of case.  The `$.expr.createPseudo` function is used to create the selector function. The selector function receives the element as an argument and returns `true` if the element matches the custom criteria, and `false` otherwise."},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Custom selectors provide a powerful way to extend jQuery's selection capabilities and target elements based on complex or application-specific criteria. Remember to define your custom selectors before using them in your jQuery code."}]
